# ─── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# It did not, until 2026-09-13, and that was harmless for exactly as long as this repository
# held no binary assets of its own. `public/` contained only `boundaries/` (JSON), so there was
# nothing for a line-ending rule to corrupt and nothing for a diff to mangle.
#
# That stopped being true when the two institutional marks landed in `frontend/public/logos/`:
# a 27 KB indexed PNG and a 99 KB SVG. Git auto-detected the PNG as binary and stored both
# blobs byte-for-byte (verified: md5 of the committed blobs equals md5 of the source files) —
# but auto-detection is a heuristic applied at add time, and `core.autocrlf` on a contributor's
# Windows checkout is free to rewrite the SVG's line endings on the way OUT. An SVG with CRLF
# still renders, so that drift is invisible in a browser and shows up only as a file that no
# longer matches the sibling repository it was copied from — which is precisely the check
# `e2e/landing-institutions-unit.spec.ts` and any future asset-parity test would make.
#
# The rules below are the sibling repository's (`designer-portal/.gitattributes`), narrowed to
# the formats this tree actually holds. Keeping them identical is deliberate: two products that
# share assets should not disagree about how those assets are stored.

# Normalise text, and let Git decide the working-copy ending per platform.
* text=auto

# Never touch these. `binary` is `-text -diff`: no line-ending conversion, no attempt to diff.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.webp          binary
*.pdf           binary
*.docx          binary
*.xlsx          binary
*.zip           binary
*.jar           binary
*.keystore      binary
*.p12           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary
*.otf           binary
*.m4a           binary
*.mp3           binary
*.mp4           binary
*.wav           binary

# SVG is text and benefits from a real diff, but it is an ASSET here — copied between
# repositories and compared byte-for-byte — so it is pinned to LF rather than left to autocrlf.
*.svg           text eol=lf

# The Gradle wrapper must stay LF or it will not execute on a POSIX runner.
gradlew         text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf

# ─── THE VENDORED TRACE ENGINE STAYS LF EVERYWHERE ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both engines are identified by a SHA-256 per file — `frontend/lib/trace/UPSTREAM-MANIFEST.txt`
# and `android/UPSTREAM-MANIFEST-KOTLIN.txt` — and those digests are taken over RAW BYTES. Under
# `* text=auto` above, a Windows checkout rewrites the newlines on the way out, so every digest
# mismatches on every Windows machine while the files are in fact correct. A drift check that
# cries drift on a clean tree is a drift check nobody reads.
#
# MEASURED, 2026-09-14, which is why both lines are here and not just the first: the designer
# portal pins only `frontend/lib/trace/**`, so its TypeScript manifest verifies on Windows and its
# KOTLIN one cannot — 103 of 108 files mismatch there on line endings alone. That gap is what this
# second line closes, and it is worth back-porting.
#
# `docs/fixtures/` is included because :core-pipeline's ParityTest reads those three JSON files as
# the shared ground truth between the Kotlin and the TypeScript; they are hashed in the Kotlin
# manifest too.
frontend/lib/trace/**   text eol=lf
android/core-imaging/** text eol=lf
android/core-vector/**  text eol=lf
android/core-pipeline/** text eol=lf
android/core-export/**  text eol=lf
docs/fixtures/**        text eol=lf
